an historic Maryland city is under state  of emergency after powerful flash  flooding devastated its downtown  torrential rain sent water rushing into  Ellicott City yesterday for the second  time in less than two years the water  approached the second floor of buildings  and swept away cars first responders  rescued dozens of people one person is  missing this morning Jeff bagasse is in  Ellicott City just west of Baltimore  Jeff good morning good morning rescue  crews work through the night after  yesterday's flooding and now they are  searching for this missing person they  do not have a lot to go on but they are  hopeful this was another devastating  flood take a look behind me at this  parking lot which is on the edge of Main  Street there are more than a dozen cars  caked in mud some overturned the flash  flood came in with a roar Sunday turning  Main Street and Ellicott City Maryland  into a raging river the fast-moving  water quickly swept away cars and  carried debris down the usually busy  Main Street as the flooding began Sunday  police had a stern warning for those  trying to drive through the high water  hole lower level is under water in some  buildings the flood waters rose past the  first floor leaving people scrambling  for higher ground actually no a lot of  people that work down here and a lot of  them were trapped on like the second and  third storey it floors because they  couldn't get out they say this is a once  every thousand year flood and we had two  of them in two years Ellicott City is  still reeling from a catastrophic 2016  flood that killed two people decimated  Main Street and cost around 20 million  dollars in damage I don't think I'm  without exaggeration saying that this is  worse than July 30th of 2016 now less  than two years later the scene here is  eerily similar to July of 2016 I can  just tell you my heart is broken  thinking about what the people are going  through here they rebuilt and now  they're faced with the same daunting  task again  they are marking up these cars a sign  that they are a total loss about two  weeks ago Maryland's governor announced  a 1 million dollar FEMA reward to help  reduce the risk of flooding also  recently officials announced that 96  percent of the businesses affected by  the flooding two years ago and reopen  their doors but this flooding once again  as raised doubts here about the future  be honest stunning images let's hope  they find that missing resident Jeff  thank you
 

